==Trivia==
License plates with low serial numbers have been distributed by politicians, as a way of rewarding supporters or associates, since the state first issued plates in 1904. Such plates have become a status symbol, similar to [[Vehicle registration plates of Delaware|Delaware]]'s low-numbered plates.<ref>{{cite web |last=Irazabal |first=Jordan |title=The Delaware 3000 |url=http://www.thedelaware3000.org/ |publisher=TheDelaware3000.org |access-date=November 25, 2014}}</ref>

Additionally, an official lottery for so-called "preferred plates" was implemented in 1995 through the Governor's Office.<ref>{{cite web |title=Plates: Overview |url=http://www.dmv.ri.gov/plates/overview/ |publisher=RI Division of Motor Vehicles |access-date=November 24, 2014}}</ref> Passenger plates with serials consisting of two letters and up to two digits, one letter and up to three digits, or all-numeric with up to five digits, are considered "preferred plates", as are commercial, motorcycle and combination plates with serials up to four digits, war veterans' plates with serials up to three digits, and [[Rhode Island National Guard|National Guard]] plates with serials up to two digits.

Rhode Island is also one of the few states that allows plate owners to transfer their plates to other family members in their [[Will and testament|will]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Transfer at Death |url=http://www.dmv.ri.gov/registrations/deathTransfer/index.php |publisher=RI Division of Motor Vehicles |access-date=November 24, 2014}}</ref>

Rhode Island  reissues plate numbers to new owners if that plate number is a inactive plate not registered in several years.

